Sure! Here's a casual and simple Java method that takes a `List<Car>` and returns the **count** and **color** of the most popular car color:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class CarColorCounter {

    public static Map.Entry<String, Long> getMostPopularColor(List<Car> cars) {
        if (cars == null || cars.isEmpty()) {
            return null; // or return a default value like Map.entry("none", 0L)
        }

        Map<String, Long> colorCount = cars.stream()
                .filter(Objects::nonNull)
                .map(Car::getColor)
                .filter(Objects::nonNull)
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Function.identity(), Collectors.counting()));

        return colorCount.entrySet().stream()
                .max(Map.Entry.comparingByValue())
                .orElseThrow(); // Or handle empty case
    }
}
```

### Example usage:

```java
public class Car {
    private String color;

    public Car(String color) {
        this.color = color;
    }

    public String getColor() {
        return color;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Car> cars = Arrays.asList(
                new Car("red"),
                new Car("blue"),
                new Car("red"),
                new Car("green"),
                new Car("blue"),
                new Car("blue")
        );

        Map.Entry<String, Long> result = CarColorCounter.getMostPopularColor(cars);
        if (result != null) {
            System.out.println("Most popular color: " + result.getKey() + ", Count: " + result.getValue());
        } else {
            System.out.println("No cars found.");
        }
    }
}
```

### Output:
```
Most popular color: blue, Count: 3
```

